B("y8revenglish/punctuation", `
m1|Which needs a comma? 'However I still want to go.'|However, I still want to go.|However I, still want to go.|However I still, want to go.|However I still want, to go.|Commas follow sentence adverbs like 'However'.
m1|Which sentence is correct?|I bought apples, pears and plums.|I bought apples pears, and plums.|I bought, apples pears and plums.|I bought apples, pears, and, plums.|Commas separate list items.
m1|Which uses a dash correctly?|We had one rule — never be late.|We had — one rule never be late.|— We had one rule never be late.|We had one rule never — be late.|A dash introduces a dramatic point.
m1|Which sentence is correct?|Before the bell rang, we packed away.|Before the bell, rang we packed away.|Before, the bell rang we packed away.|Before the bell rang we, packed away.|Comma after the subordinate clause.
m2|Where should the commas go? 'My friend Ava who loves books came over.'|My friend Ava, who loves books, came over.|My friend, Ava who loves books came over.|My friend Ava who, loves books, came over.|My friend Ava who loves books, came, over.|Commas go around the extra information.
m2|Which is correct speech punctuation?|"Wait for me," said Leo.|"Wait for me" said Leo.|"Wait for me", said Leo.|Wait for me, "said Leo."|The comma sits inside the speech marks.
m2|Which sentence uses brackets in the same way as a pair of dashes?|The castle (built in 1200) still stands.|The castle built (in 1200 still) stands.|(The castle) built in 1200 still stands.|The castle built in 1200 (still stands.|Brackets, dashes and commas can all add parenthesis.
m2|Which sentence avoids a comma splice?|The bus was late; we ran to school.|The bus was late, we ran to school.|The bus, was late we ran to school.|The bus was late we, ran to school.|A semicolon can join two related main clauses.
m2|Which needs commas around the name? 'Yes Mum I'll tidy my room.'|Yes, Mum, I'll tidy my room.|Yes Mum, I'll tidy my room.|Yes, Mum I'll tidy my room.|Yes Mum I'll, tidy my room.|Commas around a name being spoken to.
m2|Which uses a comma correctly with a conjunction?|I wanted to go, but it was raining.|I wanted, to go but it was raining.|I wanted to go but, it was raining.|I, wanted to go but it was raining.|A comma can come before 'but' joining two clauses.
m3|Which sentence is the most effective use of a dash for suspense?|She turned the handle slowly — the room was empty.|She — turned the handle slowly the room was empty.|She turned — the handle slowly the room was empty.|She turned the handle slowly the room — was empty.|The dash delays the reveal.
m3|Why is this sentence wrong? 'I love cooking my family and my dog.'|It needs commas: 'I love cooking, my family and my dog.'|It needs a dash only|It's correct|It needs speech marks|Commas change meaning.
m3|Which uses a colon correctly?|You need three things: a pen, a ruler and a rubber.|You need: three things a pen, a ruler and a rubber.|You: need three things.|You need three things a pen: a ruler.|A colon introduces a list after a complete clause.
m3|Which sentence uses commas to separate adjectives correctly?|It was a long, cold, lonely night.|It was a long cold, lonely, night.|It was, a long cold lonely night.|It was a long cold lonely, night.|Commas separate adjectives of the same kind.
m3|What does the dash add in: 'He said he'd tidy up — he didn't.'?|A contrast or twist|A list|A question|Speech|The dash sets up surprise.
m3|Which is correct?|Sadly, the match was cancelled.|Sadly the, match was cancelled.|Sadly the match, was cancelled.|Sadly the match was, cancelled.|Comma after a fronted adverb.
m3|In 'Tom — the tallest boy in our class — scored', what could replace the dashes?|Commas or brackets|Full stops|Question marks|Speech marks|All three can mark parenthesis.
t2|A comma splice joins two full sentences with only a comma.|T|Use a full stop, semicolon, dash or conjunction instead.
x2|What is the name for extra information added in brackets, dashes or commas?|parenthesis|The sentence still makes sense without it.
`);
